{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788190984,"nonce":"f4EB18592eu2eojhLv7mvABjvsc=","message":"BUILT, NOT VERIFIED. Branch `spec/manual-work-priorities`, 4 commits, clean\n`-c Release` (0 warnings). Stays open until the orchestrator runs the bench.\n\nShipped as `work-priorities {manual:true|false}` — an argument on the existing\nverb, not a new op, so `verbs` is unchanged. The justification is the repo's own\nrule applied to a lever: `MainTabWindow_Work.DoManualPrioritiesCheckbox` draws\nthis checkbox at (5,5) of the SAME window whose body is the priority matrix, so\nthe split follows where the GAME puts the control. It also settles the other 23\n`PlaySettings` fields by the same principle instead of one at a time.\n\nTHE FINDING THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FEATURE — and it extends the doctrine.\n\nDESIGN's rule is \"the gate lives in the widget\". This checkbox HAS no gate; it\nis unconditional. What it has is a SIDE EFFECT, and that turns out to be the\nload-bearing half: it fans `Pawn_WorkSettings.Notify_UseWorkPrioritiesChanged()`\nout to every player-faction pawn with non-null `workSettings`, which sets\n`workGiversDirty`, and `WorkGiversInOrderNormal`/`Emergency` — the lists\n`JobGiver_Work` actually walks — rebuild ONLY when that flag is set.\n\n**So a bare field write leaves the flip inert** until some unrelated `SetPriority`\nhappens to dirty the cache. Silent, delayed, and wrong. The result now publishes\n`manual.pawns_notified` so an inert flip is visible rather than assumed.\n\nThat is new ground: the doctrine as written covers a widget's PRECONDITIONS. A\nwidget whose citation-worthy content is its EFFECT is a second case, and it is\nnow recorded in DESIGN.\n\nTHE FLIP DESTROYS NOTHING — confirmed, and worth stating because the issue asked.\n`useWorkPriorities` is a read-time mask only: `GetPriority` returns 3 for any\nnon-zero priority when it is off, `SetPriority` writes raw either way, and\n`ExposeData` scribes the raw `DefMap`. A stored 1 survives off-and-back-on.\nWhat DOES destroy data is a write WHILE off — vanilla's own checkbox column can\nonly write 0 or 3, so such a write flattens a stored 1/2/4. The verb says both.\n\nAlso noted, not acted on: the mask is gated on `RaceProps.Humanlike`, so a\nBiotech mech's priorities read raw regardless of the setting.\n\nBOTH HALVES OF THE ACCEPTANCE, not one. 3.4's `.py` and `.ps1` gain a step 0.5\nprecondition staging the flip, so 4.7a-e and 5.1a-c are finally reachable on a\nself-staged colony — that was the whole point of this issue. Two stale claims in\n3.4's `.md` were corrected in passing. Plus `accept/manual-work-priorities.md`,\n19 envelopes, whose step 8 is the only one that can falsify the losslessness\nclaim: turn off, read a flat 3, turn on, and the stored 1 must reappear.\n\nMERGE NOTE FOR ME: this branch and `spec/stable-pawn-order` both edit\n`accept/3.4-pawn-orders.{md,ps1,py}` and `DESIGN.md`. The `.py`/`.ps1`\nauto-merge; `DESIGN.md` and the `.md` conflict, and both are ADDITIVE (two\ndecisions-log entries, two fixture-table rows) so the resolution is to keep\nboth. That collision is my dispatch error — I sent two workers at overlapping\nfiles, which the orchestration prompt explicitly warns against. Nothing was\nlost because each had its own worktree.\n\nOwes a `Build:` commit at merge; the DLL is deliberately uncommitted.","files":null}]}